+### JSON Merge Patch — `applyMergePatch(target, patch)`
+
+Applies a [JSON Merge Patch](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7386) — the format of
+`application/merge-patch+json`, in which the patch is a document shaped like the one it changes — and
+returns the merged document. It is the format for the partial updates clients send when they can
+simply describe the document they want.
+
+```ts
+import { applyMergePatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const stored = { name: 'disk', labels: { tier: 'cold', region: 'eu' }, sizes: [1, 2] };
+
+applyMergePatch(stored, {
+  labels: { tier: 'hot', region: null }, // merged member by member; null removes "region"
+  sizes: [3],                            // an array is replaced whole, never merged
+}); // { name: 'disk', labels: { tier: 'hot' }, sizes: [3] }
+
+stored; // unchanged
+```
+
+The rule is the `MergePatch` algorithm of RFC 7386 § 2, applied recursively: an object patch merges
+into an object target, member by member — a member the target lacks is added, one it has is merged
+into, and one the patch sets to `null` is removed — while a patch that is **not** an object replaces
+the target entirely, and an object patch applied to a non-object target discards it and starts from
+an empty object.
+
+As with `applyPatch`, the result is a **new value**: neither `target` nor `patch` is changed, and
+nothing is shared between the three.
+
+**Merging cannot fail**, so `applyMergePatch` throws nothing and has no error class: RFC 7386 gives
+every pair of JSON values a result. Removing a member that is not there removes nothing; adding one
+the target lacks adds it. The format pays for that simplicity by what it cannot express:
+
+| it cannot                                 | because                                                      |
+| ----------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
+| set a member to `null`                    | `null` means "remove this member"                            |
+| change one element of an array            | a non-object patch replaces its target, so arrays go whole   |
+| state a precondition, or demand a member exist | there is nothing in the format to say it with           |
+
+Use `applyPatch` when an update needs any of those three: a `test` operation, an array index, or an
+explicit `null`.
+
+RFC 7386 is obsoleted by [RFC 7396](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7396), which reprints it with
+the indentation of the § 2 pseudo-code fixed — in RFC 7386 `return Target` is misaligned into the
+loop over the patch's members. The two agree on the algorithm and on every example of their appendix
+A, and `applyMergePatch` conforms to both.
+
