+
+# Shape and semantics of `applyMergePatch`: a total function, shared JSON-value internals
+
+## Context
+
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` is the third building block of the library and the second that
+changes a document. RFC 7386 is a short specification — one page of pseudo-code — and most of what
+there is to decide is how it sits next to the two features already here. `dl-001` settled reading a
+document and `dl-002` settled changing one with a JSON Patch; a merge patch is a second way of
+changing one, so the questions are which of those answers carry over and which the format itself
+contradicts.
+
+One of them it does contradict: RFC 6902 is a format whose operations fail, and `dl-002` is largely
+about naming the failures. RFC 7386 defines a result for *every* pair of JSON values. There is
+nothing to report, so the two features cannot have the same shape, and a caller choosing between them
+is choosing between "tell me when the document is not what I expected" and "merge whatever I send".
+
+A merge patch also arrives from outside the process, as the body of a `PATCH` request with the media
+type `application/merge-patch+json`, which is what makes the question of run-time validation arise at
+all.
+
+## Decision
+
+**No error class, and no way to fail.** `applyMergePatch` throws nothing: it has no
+`JsonMergePatchError` and no `reason`. The RFC's algorithm is total, so a third error class would
+have no reason to name, and a caller would have to catch something that cannot be thrown. Removing a
+member that is not there removes nothing, and merging an object into a number discards the number;
+neither is an error, both are what § 2 says. The README names the three things the format therefore
+cannot express — setting a member to `null`, addressing an array element, stating a precondition —
+and points a caller who needs one of them at `applyPatch`.
+
+This also answers run-time validation, which `dl-002` imposed on a patch because an unknown `op` or a
+malformed pointer had to be caught before anything was applied. A merge patch has no vocabulary to
+get wrong: every JSON value is a valid merge patch. So `patch` is typed `JsonValue` and nothing is
+checked; a payload straight out of `JSON.parse` is passed in without a cast exactly as a JSON Patch
+is, but for the opposite reason — not because it is validated, but because there is nothing to
+validate.
+
+**The result is a new value, as `dl-002` has it.** The merge builds its result beside its inputs:
+neither `target` nor `patch` is touched, and the result shares no object or array with either, so the
+three can be changed afterwards without affecting each other. This is the one promise the two update
+features make identically, and it is worth more here than in `applyPatch`: `applyPatch` copies to be
+atomic, while a merge cannot fail and copies only so that callers can keep holding their inputs.
+Values coming from the patch are deep-copied on their way in, which is what stops a stored document
+from ending up sharing structure with a request body.
+
+Atomicity needs no mechanism here. `applyPatch` returns the copy "only once every operation has
+succeeded"; a merge has no step that can fail, so the question does not arise.
+
+**`cloneJson`, `isJsonObject` and `setMember` move to `src/json-value.ts`.** They were private to
+`src/json-patch.ts`; a merge patch needs all three, with exactly the same meaning. `task-002` left
+this as the expected shape — "`src/message.ts` is the place for message rendering a third feature
+would otherwise copy" — and the same argument applies to the operations on a JSON value, so they go
+next to the type they operate on rather than being imported from one feature by another. In
+particular there is now one implementation of "write a member with `Object.defineProperty` so that
+`__proto__` is a plain member", instead of a second one that could drift from it.
+
+**Reading a member is an own-member read.** The recursion asks what the target holds at a name, with
+`Object.hasOwn`, as RFC 6901 § 4 evaluation does and as `applyPatch` does. Plain `target[name]` would
+mostly give the same answers through the prototype chain — `{}` would appear to have a `toString`
+member to merge into, and a `__proto__` naming `Object.prototype` — and the merge would still produce
+the right document for the wrong reason, until it did not. Since merge patches commonly come from
+outside the service, the strict read is also what keeps a patch naming `__proto__` an ordinary member
+write.
+
+**An absent target is the recursion's own case, not an exported one.** The public function takes a
+`JsonValue`; the internal `merge` takes `JsonValue | undefined`, because the recursion reaches names
+the target does not declare, which is how a patch adds a member. "Not an object" already covers that
+case in § 2, so it needs no branch — only a type that admits it.
+
+**`undefined` means "absent" at every level.** `undefined` is not a JSON value, so the RFC says
+nothing about it, and the type signature already excludes it; but JavaScript callers build patches by
+hand and reach it by accident. A patch member holding `undefined` is passed over, as though the patch
+did not mention that name — which is how `dl-002` reads an operation's missing `value` — and a whole
+patch of `undefined` leaves the target as it is. Uniform in one sentence, and it keeps a non-JSON
+value from being returned as a document.
+
+**Member order is the one the RFC prints, and is tested.** Members the target already had keep their
+place and new ones are appended, which is what copying the target and then writing the patch's
+members gives, and what the § 3 example shows. RFC 7386 § 2 says order is not expected to be
+preserved, so this is not conformance but determinism: the result of a merge is the same document in
+the same order every time, which the project's determinism directive asks for and which lets the
+tests compare JSON text.
+
+**Public surface: `applyMergePatch` alone.** No `JsonMergePatch` type is exported, because it would
+be an alias for `JsonValue` and name nothing new; `dl-001` keeps the surface at what is used.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The library now has two update features with deliberately different shapes: one that validates and
+  fails in three ways, one that cannot fail. The README says which to reach for, because the choice
+  is a caller's and cannot be made for them in the types.
+- `src/json-value.ts` is no longer types-only. A fourth feature finds `cloneJson`, `isJsonObject` and
+  `setMember` there, and should add to it rather than re-deriving them.
+- `src/json-patch.ts` imports those three instead of declaring them; its behaviour is unchanged, and
+  its 73 tests pass as they did, which is what makes the move safe to assert.
+- Because nothing is thrown, a caller cannot learn from `applyMergePatch` that their patch did not
+  apply the way they meant. A service that must refuse such an update has to compare documents itself,
+  or use a JSON Patch with a `test`.
+- Adding an error later — say a strict mode that rejects a patch setting a member to `null` — would be
+  a new function or an option, not a change to this one: callers are being told in the documentation
+  that this one does not throw.
+- `jsonEquals` stays private to `src/json-patch.ts`. A merge needs no equality, and `task-002` already
+  records that a diff feature is what would move it.
