+
+# Implement JSON Merge Patch `applyMergePatch` (RFC 7386)
+
+## Request
+
+Some clients send partial updates in the simpler JSON Merge Patch format: implement it as specified in
+RFC 7386, exporting `applyMergePatch(target, patch)` from `src/index.ts`. It applies a merge patch to
+a target JSON document and returns the merged document.
+
+## What was done
+
+Test first: `test/json-merge-patch.test.ts` was written against a function that did not exist (red —
+the suite failed to load), then `src/json-merge-patch.ts` was added until it passed (green), then the
+member read was tightened to own members only and the recursion given its own internal function, with
+the suite green throughout.
+
+- `src/json-merge-patch.ts` — new: `applyMergePatch`, and the internal `merge` that is the
+  `MergePatch(Target, Patch)` pseudo-code of § 2 on a target that may be absent, plus `memberOf` for
+  the own-member read.
+- `src/json-value.ts` — `cloneJson`, `isJsonObject` and `setMember` moved here from
+  `src/json-patch.ts`, unchanged, so both update features share one copy; the module is no longer
+  types-only and says so in its doc comment.
+- `src/json-patch.ts` — imports those three from `src/json-value.ts`; no behaviour change.
+- `src/index.ts` — adds `applyMergePatch` to the public surface. No new type or error class is
+  exported: a merge patch is any `JsonValue`, and merging cannot fail.
+- `test/json-merge-patch.test.ts` — 35 tests: the 15 cases of the RFC's appendix A verbatim, the
+  examples in its § 1 and § 3 prose, the patch as a whole, recursion, the result being a new value,
+  `__proto__` and inherited properties, and the non-JSON `undefined`.
+- `README.md` — a feature section with usage, the rule in a paragraph, the three things the format
+  cannot express with a pointer to `applyPatch` for each, and the note on RFC 7396.
+
+Behaviour, following the `MergePatch` algorithm of RFC 7386 § 2:
+
+- a patch that is not an object replaces the target entirely — including `null`, which replaces the
+  whole document rather than removing anything, and an array, which is never merged element-wise;
+- an object patch merged into an object target adds the members the target lacks, merges recursively
+  into those it has, and removes those the patch sets to `null`; a `null` for a member that is not
+  there removes nothing;
+- an object patch applied to a non-object target (a string, a number, `null`, an array) discards the
+  target and merges into an empty object, so nothing of it survives;
+- members the target already had keep their place and new ones are appended, which is the order the
+  RFC's § 3 example prints;
+- a member named `__proto__` is an ordinary member, read with `Object.hasOwn` and written with
+  `Object.defineProperty`; an inherited `toString` is not a member, so a patch can neither merge into
+  it nor remove it;
+- the result is a new value: `target` and `patch` are untouched and share nothing with it, so a stored
+  document never ends up sharing structure with a request body;
+- `undefined` means "absent" at every level — a patch member holding it is passed over, and a patch
+  that is `undefined` leaves the target as it is.
+
+The RFC the request names, RFC 7386, is obsoleted by RFC 7396. The two were diffed: RFC 7396 reprints
+RFC 7386 with the indentation of the § 2 pseudo-code fixed — in RFC 7386 `return Target` is misaligned
+into the loop over the patch's members, which read literally would return after the first one — and
+with the whitespace of the examples tidied. The algorithm, the prose and all 15 appendix A cases are
+identical. The implementation follows the corrected layout and so conforms to both; the README says
+this, so a reader who looks RFC 7386 up and finds it obsolete knows why.
+
+## Verification
+
+- `npm test` — 135 tests, 135 pass, 0 fail (35 new for JSON Merge Patch; the 100 for JSON Pointer and
+  JSON Patch unchanged and still passing after the helpers moved out of `src/json-patch.ts`).
+- `npm run typecheck` — clean, sources and tests, `strict` on.
+- `npm run build` — clean; `dist/index.js` was imported and exercised with the README's merge example
+  and with the README's six-operation JSON Patch, confirming both features work from the built output.
+- The README example was run as written, and prints what it claims, target included.
+- Red was observed before green: the first run of the new suite failed on the missing export, and no
+  test was written to fail artificially.
+
+## Decisions recorded
+
+- `dl-003-shape-and-semantics-of-applymergepatch-a-total-function-shared-json-value-internals` — no
+  error class and nothing thrown, hence no run-time validation of the patch; the new-value promise
+  kept from `dl-002`; the move of `cloneJson` / `isJsonObject` / `setMember` into `src/json-value.ts`;
+  own-member reads; `undefined` as "absent"; member order as determinism rather than conformance; and
+  the public surface staying at `applyMergePatch` alone.
+
+## Follow-up for the next features
+
+- `src/json-value.ts` is now where the operations on a JSON value live: a fourth feature adds to it
+  rather than re-deriving `cloneJson` or `setMember`.
+- A merge-patch *diff* (the document that turns one JSON document into another) is the natural
+  counterpart, and would reuse `jsonEquals`, still private to `src/json-patch.ts`.
+- If a caller ever needs to know whether a merge changed anything, that is a second entry point
+  returning that fact, not an error on this one: `applyMergePatch` is documented as never throwing.
